 * cite68†Programs L110:     * cite69†Bay Delta Program L111:     * cite70†Cannabis Cultivation L112:     * cite30†Drought Information & Updates L113:     * cite71†Applications L114:     * cite72†Fees L115:     * cite73†Hearings L116:     * cite74†Decisions L117:     * cite75†Petitions & Transfers L118:     * cite76†Public Notices L119: \nL120:     * cite77†Public Trust L121:     * cite78†Registrations L122:     * cite79†Revocation Information L123:     * cite80†Reports L124:     * cite68†More Programs . . . L125: \nL126:   * cite81† Water Boards  L127:     * cite82†State Water Resources Control Board L128:       * cite81†Water Boards Map L129:       * cite43†Division of Drinking Water L130:       * cite33†Division of Financial Assistance L131:       * cite20†Division of Water Quality L132:       * cite67†Division of Water Rights L133: \nL134:     * cite83†Region 1 - North Coast L135:     * cite84†Region 2 - San Francisco Bay L136:     * cite85†Region 3 - Central Coast L137:     * cite86†Region 4 - Los Angeles L138:     * cite87†Region 5 - Central Valley L139:     * cite88†Region 6 - Lahontan L140:     * cite89†Region 7 - Colorado River Basin L141:     * cite90†Region 8 - Santa Ana L142:     * cite91†Region 9 - San Diego L143:     * cite92†My Water Quality†mywaterquality.ca.gov L144:     * cite93†Santa Monica Bay Restoration†www.smbrc.ca.gov L145:     * cite94†CalEPA†calepa.ca.gov L146: \nL147: Welcome to the Construction Stormwater Program\nL148: The California State Water Resources Control Board (State Water Board) regulates stormwater discharges from construction sites because of its potential to mobilize pollutants and discharge into waterbodies or watersheds. By regulating these discharges, the State Water Board is preserving, enhancing, and restoring California's waterbodies and its resources.\nL149: Construction activity subject to this permit includes clearing, grading and disturbances to the ground such as stockpiling, or excavation, but does not include regular maintenance activities performed to restore the original line, grade, or capacity of the facility.\nL150: Dischargers whose projects disturb one (1) or more acres of soil or whose projects disturb less than one acre but are part of a larger common plan of development that in total disturbs one or more acres, are required to obtain coverage under the National Pollutant Discharge Elimination System (NPDES) General Permit for Stormwater Discharges Associated with Construction and Land Disturbance Activities.\nL151: ##   Announcements\nL152: \nL153:   * NEW! cite95†2022 CGP Qualifying Precipitation Event Guidance L154:   * cite96†Sites directly impacted by the Los Angeles and other wildfires L155: \nL156: ##   Construction Stormwater General Permits\nL157: \nL158: ### 2022 Construction Stormwater General Permit\nL159: \nL160: The State Water Board adopted the 2022 Construction Stormwater General Permit, Order 2022-0057-DWQ, on September 8, 2022, and it went into effect on September 1, 2023.\nL161: \nL162: cite97†Construction Stormwater General Permit Order 2022-0057-DWQ L163: ### Lake Tahoe Basin Construction Stormwater General Permit\nL164: \nL165: The Lahontan Regional Water Board adopted a general NPDES permit to regulate stormwater discharges from construction activity in the Lake Tahoe Hydrologic Unit. Owners of construction projects in this watershed must apply for the Regional Water Board permit rather than the statewide permit.\nL166: \nL167: cite98†Construction Stormwater General Permit for the Lake Tahoe Basin Order R6T-2016-0010 L168: \nL169: cite99†Image: Image Description #1 L170: ## SMARTS Login\nL171: \nL172: SMARTS provides a platform where dischargers, regulators, and the public can enter, manage, and view data associated with California's Stormwater General Permits.\nL173: \nL174: cite100†Visit SMARTS†smarts.waterboards.ca.gov L175: \nL176: cite101†Image: Image Description #3 L177: ## Water Quality Fees\nL178: \nL179: Find information regarding water quality fees for NPDES Stormwater Permits, pay invoice(s) online (EFT or credit card), find our tax ID number, and find fee related information for stakeholders.\nL180: \nL181: cite102†Learn About Fees cite103†Pay Invoices L182: \nL183: cite104†Image: stormwater icon L184: ## Report a Stormwater Violation\nL185: \nL186: Inform the Water Boards about potential permit violations or water quality concerns resulting from construction activities.\nL187: \nL188: cite105†CalEPA Environmental Complaint System†calepa.my.salesforce-sites.com L189: \nL190: ##   Stay informed\nL191: \nL192: ### Email Subscription List\nL193: \nL194: To receive updates on the Construction Stormwater Program, subscribe to the \"Stormwater Construction Permitting Issues\" email list.\nL195: \nL196: [Input] [Input] cite106†Image†content.govdelivery.com Email Address [Input]\nL197: \nL198: [Input]\nL199: ### Contact Us\nL200: \nL201:   * General Inquiries:\nL202: stormwater@waterboards.ca.gov\nL203: Toll Free: 1-(866)-563-3107\nL204:   * cite107†State and Regional Board Contact Information L205: \nL206:   (Page last updated 11/17/2025)\nL207: #### Storm Water Resources\nL208: \nL209:   * cite41†Stormwater Home L210:   * cite108†Caltrans L211:   * cite109†Construction L212: \nL213:   * cite107†Contacts L214:   * cite110†Databases L215:   * cite111†Historical L216: \nL217:   * cite112†Industrial L218:   * cite113†Municipal L219:   * cite114†SMARTS Resources L220: \nL221:   * cite115†STORMS L222:   * cite116†Training L223:   * cite117†Trash Implementation L224: \nL225: Water is a precious resource in California, and maintaining its quality is of utmost importance to safeguard the health of the public and the environment.\nL226: #### Statewide Campaigns\nL227: \nL228:   * cite118† EPA Water Sense†www.epa.gov L229:   * ci